$DIR/main.p:7:2: cannot store E{...} in package variable Err, which outlives main's frame
    Converting a value larger than one word to an interface puts a copy of the
    value in the frame of the function that converts it (here main, at
    main.p:7), and the interface points at that copy. When main returns, its
    frame is reclaimed, but package variables live for the whole program, so
    package variable Err would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value itself instead of a view of main's frame, or allocate it so
    that it lives as long as package variable Err.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
